AGENTS.md
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc
ai-csuite/ai-csuite-framework-prd.md
/.tmp-claude-code-security-review-ref
*.zip
wip/
skills.yaml
skills.yaml.*-bak-*

# Per-runtime local tooling state. These directories are generated by the
# agent runtimes themselves (installed skill links, local permission
# overrides, model defaults) and describe this machine, not the project.
.agents/
.claude/
.kiro/
opencode.json
# The real global agent rules are kept in a separate private repo and are
# reachable from here through a symlink. Do NOT collapse these back into a
# bare `rules/`: that pattern matches only a directory, so it stops covering
# the path the moment it is a link, and the rules become committable here.
# The backup patterns catch local copies too. Only rules.example/ is public.
rules
rules.bak*
rules.pre-symlink-*

# ai-csuite company profiles hold real business context (revenue, runway,
# legal entity, constraints). Keep them local; only company.yaml ships as
# the worked example.
skills/ai-csuite/config/*.yaml
!skills/ai-csuite/config/company.yaml

# Trellis session handoffs quote the incidents they came from: production
# hostnames, deployment instance names, credential-entry names. That context
# is what makes a handoff useful, and exactly what must not ship in a public
# repo. The workspace journal is de-identified by hand and stays tracked.
.trellis/handoffs/

# Task artifacts and code-specs are working notes for one developer's session:
# PRDs, research dumps, verification logs, provider scores. No task or spec file
# has ever been tracked here, so this only makes the existing convention
# explicit and stops a stray `git add .` from committing them. Trade-off: the
# Phase 3.3 code-spec knowledge stays local instead of accumulating in the repo.
.trellis/tasks/
.trellis/spec/

# Per-repository worktree runtime link created by the shared-memory skill. It
# points into the git common dir and is machine-local by construction.
.trellis/shared
